>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `[iff]`

``` hol4
Theorem theorem_name[iff]: statement
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Registers a theorem persistently as both a simplification and classical
reasoning rule.

The argument-free `[iff]` theorem attribute adds its theorem to `srw_ss()`
and derives temporary-claset-style rules according to its conclusion:

| Conclusion after antecedents | Classical rule |
|---|---|
| `A <=> B` | introduction from `B` and destruction from `A` |
| `~A` | elimination from `A` |
| any other `A` | introduction of `A` |

The source theorem is universally specialized before classification. A
derived rule is safe exactly when the source conclusion has no implication
antecedents; otherwise it is unsafe. For an equivalence, the major premise
is placed before the source antecedents.

The declaration is inherited through theory ancestry. Its claset and
simpset views are rebuilt when declarations are replayed. Because `[intro]`
and `[iff]` use separate declaration streams, their relative recency can
change across reload when otherwise equal candidates tie; rule meaning and
priority are unaffected.

The simpset view is an ordinary named rewrite. Its deletion key is the
declaring theorem's `Thy.name`, so `Excl "Thy.name"`, `delsimps`, and
`temp_delsimps` affect it exactly as they affect a rewrite declared with
`[simp]`; none of those simpset-only controls removes the classical view.

A theory's `[iff]` rewrites form a simpset fragment of their own, named
`Thy-iff`, distinct from the `Thy` fragment carrying its `[simp]`
declarations. `ExclSF`, `diminish_srw_ss` and `exclude_ssfrags` therefore
address the two streams separately.

Only a delsimps that names the rewrite in qualified form is persistent. A
bare `delsimps ["name"]` matches any rewrite of that name from any theory,
so it is read as aimed at the ancestor rewrite it names, and it reaches a
same-named local `[iff]` declaration for the current session only. Use
`clasimpLib.remove_iff "name"` to retract a declaration persistently, or
`delsimps ["Thy.name.__clasimp_iff"]` to retract just its rewrite. Use
`Iff th` for the same two derived views during one clasimp tactic
invocation only.

### Failure

The attribute takes no arguments. Registration can fail if the theorem
cannot be converted to the required rewrite or classical-rule form.

### Example

``` hol4
Theorem iff_example[iff]:
  p /\ T <=> p
Proof
  simp []
QED
```

